WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.060
Speaker 1: Today.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:04.870
We're going to be speaking with Dan Cannon,
who's a civil rights lawyer and a law professor

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.140
at the University of Louisville.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:12.160
He's also author of the book Pleading Out
How Plea Bargaining Creates a Permanent Criminal

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:13.160
Class.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.160
Dan, really great having you on.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.160
I appreciate your time.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.209
Speaker 4: Hi, David.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:17.209
Thanks for having.

00:00:17.209 --> 00:00:18.209
Speaker 1: Me.

00:00:18.209 --> 00:00:21.890
So, I mean, I think maybe to start it would
it would make sense to talk about how this

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:25.900
sort of like a plea deal industrial complex
started.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:31.849
I think many of us intuitively know just because
you've been charged with a crime doesn't mean

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:36.860
that there will eventually be a trial and
a verdict in the way that we watch sometimes

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.410
on television in major trials.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.310
How did this develop that many cases don't
make it to trial?

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:44.310
Whose motive?

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.410
Who was motivated to make that the reality?

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.410
Speaker 4: Sure.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:52.810
And I would I would go so far as to say almost
all cases don't make it to trial.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:57.900
Where we're sitting at right now is a 97%
plea bargain rate, which is, you know, just

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.660
to sort of grasp the how big that is.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.749
You know, that's that's three out of every
out of every hundred criminal cases that might

00:01:05.749 --> 00:01:06.749
go to trial.

00:01:06.749 --> 00:01:07.749
Right.

00:01:07.749 --> 00:01:08.749
You know, on average.

00:01:08.749 --> 00:01:09.749
And that's the entire country.

00:01:09.749 --> 00:01:14.270
This is way, way, way far beyond what anybody
else is doing in the rest of the world.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:21.179
The rest of the world, you know, even in comparable
legal systems, places like the United Kingdom

00:01:21.179 --> 00:01:22.299
and Canada and so on.

00:01:22.299 --> 00:01:24.369
I mean, the highest you get is to 80%.

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:26.840
And even that is kind of extreme.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:30.009
So nobody does it as much as we do.

00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:35.310
And and the result has been pretty much the
death of the jury trial altogether.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:40.770
For all intents and purposes, the community
has been completely removed from processes

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:41.889
of criminal justice.

00:01:41.889 --> 00:01:48.179
And if you look historically at how and why
this originated, you know, this uniquely American

00:01:48.179 --> 00:01:49.179
thing that we do.

00:01:49.179 --> 00:01:54.600
You got to go back all the way to the beginning
of the 19th century and look at the early

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.909
labor movement in the United States.

00:01:56.909 --> 00:02:01.130
And when that was gathering steam, that is
at the same time that that is the same time

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.649
that the plea bargain sort of took off in
New England.

00:02:03.649 --> 00:02:04.649
Right.

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:07.790
So you have this post-industrial revolution
situation where the working classes are getting

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:09.789
bigger and bigger and bigger and bigger.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:13.500
And up until 1830, you know, plea bargaining
was taboo.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:19.590
There was I mean, you would see prosecutors
actually get prosecuted for trying to make

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.500
deals with defendants in the way that they
do now.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.560
So it was completely off the table and just
wasn't happening.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.629
Or if it was happening, we didn't know about
it.

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:34.830
Then in Massachusetts, you see that number
tick up very quickly by the 1850s.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:39.470
It's 50% of all criminal cases are ending
in a guilty plea.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:41.840
By the 1880s, it's 88%.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:42.840
Right.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.939
So almost all criminal cases are ending in
a guilty plea by the end of the 19th century

00:02:47.939 --> 00:02:49.870
in Massachusetts.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:54.751
And what's happening there is that there's
this this giant explosion of working class

00:02:54.751 --> 00:02:55.751
people.

00:02:55.751 --> 00:02:56.751
Right.

00:02:56.751 --> 00:03:02.110
And at the beginning, in the 1830s, the courts
and every other American institution is working

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:05.040
really, really hard to break the back of organized
labor.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:12.129
You've got the first strikes are happening
in the 1820s, and then the first Federation

00:03:12.129 --> 00:03:16.100
of Labor Unions happens in New York in 1834.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.439
And so the ruling classes are sort of panicking
and coming, trying to figure out, you know,

00:03:20.439 --> 00:03:22.780
how to how to fix this whole thing.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:27.829
First thing they start doing is prosecuting
groups of workers under conspiracy statutes.

00:03:27.829 --> 00:03:30.849
So you get big groups of time to say, all
right, you know, you're going to get together

00:03:30.849 --> 00:03:36.390
and try to get higher wages or, you know,
a shorter workdays or something like that.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.920
We're going to prosecute you for a conspiracy.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.780
And it becomes pretty apparent early on that
that's that's not going to work by the 1840

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:44.780
days.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.820
They've sort of scrapped that whole idea because
there's just too many of them.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:52.499
And there's the working classes are getting
too big and there's working class people for

00:03:52.499 --> 00:03:56.870
the first time sitting on these criminal juries.

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:01.469
So the response of the ruling classes at that
time is, as I talk about in my book, is to,

00:04:01.469 --> 00:04:04.030
number one, take a lot of power away from
juries.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:06.609
Juries are not nearly as powerful now as they
were back then.

00:04:06.609 --> 00:04:09.390
And the other one is to introduce the plea
bargain.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:14.540
So what that allows them to do is to quickly
and quietly prosecute lots of individual workers

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:19.699
one by one, rather than trying to smack a
whole bunch of them at a time with the hammer

00:04:19.699 --> 00:04:22.560
of the criminal law, which they knew that
wasn't going to work.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:23.560
They were just too outnumbered.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:26.840
Speaker 1: In the modern era.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:28.449
What is the advantage?

00:04:28.449 --> 00:04:30.169
We'll talk first about the prosecutors.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:36.010
I mean, it seems if everybody went to trial,
they would not have nearly the work force

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:38.580
that would be required to try that number
of cases.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:39.580
Right.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:40.580
I mean, it just seems like an impossibility.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:41.580
Speaker 4: Yeah.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:42.580
Yeah.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:48.419
So what you see in situations where the in
the few situations where you've actually reduced

00:04:48.419 --> 00:04:55.789
the number of plea bargains is that it does
force prosecutors to make tough decisions.

00:04:55.789 --> 00:05:00.770
So you look at the historical example of Alaska
for a very brief period of time in Alaska,

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:06.580
you had an attorney general come in and say,
you know, this is we're going to stop doing

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:07.580
plea bargaining.

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:10.340
We're going to reduce the number of plea bargains
like all the plea bargains are going to come

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:11.340
through me.

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:12.340
Yep.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:14.960
And and what I've heard since I was in law
school is that if somebody does something

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.509
like that, it's going to crash the whole system.

00:05:17.509 --> 00:05:18.509
Right.

00:05:18.509 --> 00:05:22.729
We're just not going to have a criminal justice
system anymore because the wheels of justice

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:23.740
are going to stop turning.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:27.610
And, you know, all these jury trials are going
to make it to where we just can't do anything.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:28.610
Right.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:29.610
But that's not true.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.800
If you look at the historical example of Alaska
and some of these other places where this

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:40.690
has happened, what happened in Alaska was
lots more cases went to trial, but the system

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:41.690
didn't crash.

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:43.990
It adapted and it got more intelligent.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:50.570
So at the point in time in Alaska, where this
new attorney general took over, the Alaskan

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:55.069
prosecutors were rejecting about 4% of all
the cases that police brought to them.

00:05:55.069 --> 00:05:57.880
So cops will come in with a case and it was
a bad case.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:01.740
If it was a good case, it didn't matter because
the prosecutors knew that they could push

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.830
somebody into some kind of plea bargain and
get a conviction.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:06.979
Right.

00:06:06.979 --> 00:06:11.320
Taking plea bargaining off the table means
that the prosecutors have to be more selective.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:15.300
And so what happens right off the bat is that
the prosecutors go from rejecting 4% of all

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.370
those cases to rejecting 40% of them.

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:23.430
So that forces prosecutors to to make decisions.

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:24.430
Right.

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:27.860
To come up with priorities for what they're
going to prosecute and what they're really,

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:33.780
you know, what the community's priorities
really are.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:38.240
And and so far from crashing this system,
it adapts.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:39.680
It becomes more intelligent.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.810
The prosecutors are making more intelligent
decisions.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:47.830
And that forces the police to cross their
T's and dot their eyes and do more diligent

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:48.830
homework.

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:52.240
So if you think about the situation that we
have now, you know, police know they're going

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:53.389
to get a conviction.

00:06:53.389 --> 00:06:58.470
They know that they're going to you know,
they they out of most of the 11 million arrests

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:03.720
that happen in the United States every year
are going to result in some kind of conviction.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:07.590
And so they don't have to have any real evidence
that a crime was committed.

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:13.970
They don't have to you know, they're not anticipating
that they're ever going to be put on the stand

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:18.990
or have to account for the charges that they've
levied against somebody in any meaningful

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:20.470
way.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:26.120
And and if you reduce the number of plea bargain
cases, even just by a little bit, that whole

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:27.909
situation starts to change.

00:07:27.909 --> 00:07:31.020
Because police know that the case is more
likely to go to trial.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:34.150
And so they have to be more careful in what
they bring to prosecutors in the first place.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:38.150
Speaker 1: Now, from the standpoint of the
defendants, the accused, I think many of us

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:44.430
have this instinct that if we were ever in
such a situation and we felt that we were

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:49.180
innocent, we would fight to the end and demand
a jury trial and so on and so forth.

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:53.440
What are the incentives that are there for
those who are charged to end up taking plea

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:54.440
deals?

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.280
Speaker 4: Well, it's every incentive in the
world, isn't it?

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:59.610
You know, so so that's what we've decided
to do in the United States.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.750
That's so different from the rest of the world.

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:02.750
Here.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:08.010
We decided that we're going to give every
carrot and every stick to prosecutors so they

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.240
can end up with conviction somehow.

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:11.240
Right.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:16.409
So this was all still sorting itself out by
the late 20th century.

00:08:16.409 --> 00:08:21.191
And then there was this Supreme Court case
called Bordon Carter versus Hayes, where you

00:08:21.191 --> 00:08:26.810
have right down the road here in Lexington,
Kentucky, you've got a 29 year old black horse

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:33.099
transporter who gets picked up for trying
to cash a bad check worth $88.

00:08:33.099 --> 00:08:34.099
Right.

00:08:34.099 --> 00:08:35.329
So he writes a bad check.

00:08:35.329 --> 00:08:39.810
The prosecutor comes to him, says, well, it
looks like you were in trouble ten years ago

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:41.409
over something.

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:45.640
And so what you're going to do is for this
$88 check, you're going to take five years

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.120
in prison.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:49.300
And Paul Hayes says, I don't want to take
five years in prison.

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:54.510
Prosecutor says, Well, if you don't take five
years in prison, then I'm going to hit you

00:08:54.510 --> 00:09:00.010
with this habitual offender charge and that
carries a mandatory penalty of life in prison.

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:03.740
And he's like, I don't want to do life in
prison over an $80 check.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.430
But that's the kind of power the prosecutor
has.

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:10.670
We're still sort of sorting out whether or
not we wanted to do that.

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:14.170
In that case, Paul Hayes didn't want to take
the deal.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:18.630
He went to trial, got convicted and got a
life sentence.

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:20.490
And that case went all the way up to the Supreme
Court.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:25.410
And the Supreme Court at that time in 1978,
essentially says, yeah, you know, it's the

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:30.550
prosecutor's job to persuade a defendant to
give up their right to trial.

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:32.680
And they can do that by hook or by crook.

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:39.410
They can do whatever they want to get a defendant
to to to plead guilty and to forego the, as

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:43.060
the prosecutor says, the inconvenience of
the trial.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:49.399
And so that case sort of throws the floodgates
open for prosecutors to use anything and everything

00:09:49.399 --> 00:09:50.399
that they want.

00:09:50.399 --> 00:09:57.550
So, you know, if you have if you're charged
with murder, let's say, you know, the prosecutor

00:09:57.550 --> 00:09:58.910
can offer carrots.

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:04.029
You can say, look, you know, we're going to
bust these charges down to a wanton endangerment

00:10:04.029 --> 00:10:06.769
and you're going to do six months probation
because I just don't want to take it to trial.

00:10:06.769 --> 00:10:08.320
And I don't think it's a very good case.

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:09.459
But I need a conviction.

00:10:09.459 --> 00:10:11.040
So, you know, take it or leave it.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.279
And you'd be hard pressed to say, I'm going
to leave that on the table.

00:10:14.279 --> 00:10:21.600
Conversely, if a prosecutor wants to proceed
as though it is a death penalty case and threaten

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:27.790
a defendant with literal death if they don't
take a plea bargain to, let's say, life without

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:30.570
prison, they can do that, too.

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:33.290
And in fact, when you look at the numbers,
one of the more chilling statistics that I've

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:39.760
got in my book is that since the 1970s, 75%
of the people on death row could have avoided

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:43.690
a death sentence by taking the plea offer
that the prosecutor gave to them.

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:47.500
So in other words, the prosecutor has the
power to say, you know, if you don't take

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.170
this deal, I'm going to put the death sentence
on the table.

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.730
It's not on the table until you take it, until
you say yes or no.

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:55.720
But if you make me go to trial, well, death
penalty is on the table.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:11:01.130
So prosecutors just had this enormous power
to alter the legal landscape in any given

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:05.230
case that in the United States that they just
don't have in other countries.

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:10.200
Speaker 1: Is it easier for wealthier defendants
to avoid taking plea deals in the sense that

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:14.290
all else being equal, a long trial costs more
money?

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:15.970
Speaker 4: Sure.

00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:20.240
For for for reasons that I said before, long
trials cost more money.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.790
But they're also, you know, more high profile.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:24.930
Right.

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:27.130
They're more visible to the public.

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:30.330
The media pays more attention to what's going
on in these high profile cases.

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:37.269
So you get somebody like a Jeffrey Epstein
that's going to come in with you know, it's

00:11:37.269 --> 00:11:42.490
quite obviously is is is guilty, but comes
in with a team of, you know, 100 high paid

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:51.110
lawyers that are going to make the prosecution
work for for anything that they end up with.

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:54.290
And so the prosecutor starts to look at that
situation saying, all right, this is going

00:11:54.290 --> 00:11:56.540
to be very time consuming, is going to be
very difficult.

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:59.380
It's going to be very expensive.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:01.980
And it could be very humiliating if we lose.

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:02.980
Right.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:06.690
You know, this this this could have political
consequences and all this other stuff.

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:09.300
And it's going to be very much in the public
eye.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:16.220
So rather than go through, you know, the the
hoops of a trial, let's try to get them into

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:18.829
a place where they're going to take a plea.

00:12:18.829 --> 00:12:23.300
And in fact, that is exactly what happened
down in Florida with Jeffrey Epstein and the

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:28.660
initial disastrous prosecution they tried
to carry on their.

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:32.960
And in the victims were completely cut out
of that prosecution.

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:38.740
And Epstein got a slap on the wrist for, you
know, abhorrent conduct.

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:44.130
And that is the kind of treatment that the
wealthy typically can expect to receive in

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:45.130
the United States.

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:48.610
So when people talk about there being two
different justice systems here, that's that's

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:49.610
what they mean.

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:53.220
Speaker 1: It seems the other component to
this also is the use of pretrial detention

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:54.399
and the ability to make bail.

00:12:54.399 --> 00:13:01.110
I mean, as a very general concept, I think
it's probably more difficult to mount a defense.

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:07.300
All else being equal, if you are being detained
up until the time of your trial rather than

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:08.300
out.

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:12.900
Can you talk a little bit about that and the
effect of pretrial detention and possibly

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:18.110
the unequal application of pretrial detention
on the sort of plea bargain chances, I guess?

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:19.139
Speaker 4: Sure.

00:13:19.139 --> 00:13:24.079
So cash bail, like the plea bargain is one
of those things that we've carried forward,

00:13:24.079 --> 00:13:29.200
you know, from an overtly class system in
the 19th century until now.

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:30.200
Right.

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:34.660
So it's yet another gun that a prosecution
that the prosecution can put to a defendant's

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:38.780
head to say, like, listen, you know, if you
don't take this deal, you're just going to

00:13:38.780 --> 00:13:41.060
stay locked up there until we have a trial.

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:42.730
And that could be two years from now.

00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:44.089
That could be three years from now.

00:13:44.089 --> 00:13:45.790
So if you can't post bail.

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:46.790
Tough luck there.

00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:51.680
You sit in the county jail and these county
jails, as I'm sure you know, are oftentimes

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:52.880
not friendly places.

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:57.990
They're so unfriendly, in fact, and especially
metropolitan jails are so unfriendly that

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:02.190
you see defendants taking deals where they
end up going to prison just because prisons

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:05.730
are a little friendlier than the county jail
right now, for example.

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:08.079
You see that a lot.

00:14:08.079 --> 00:14:14.149
But but basically, it's it's this arsenal
of tools that the prosecution has.

00:14:14.149 --> 00:14:19.269
Cash bail being one of them to make sure that
that that every defendant gets pressed into

00:14:19.269 --> 00:14:21.370
taking a deal of some kind.

00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:27.690
I had a client that I represented in a civil
rights case who insisted on his innocence

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:33.920
in in these criminal charges that were levied
against him way out in rural Kentucky.

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:38.209
And he sat there in jail waiting for his trial
to come up for three years.

00:14:38.209 --> 00:14:42.860
Well, the maximum sentence that he could have
come up with, that he could have been subjected

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.980
to was only two years while on the original
charges.

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:49.960
So he sits there in three years waiting for
his trial and then goes to trial and no police

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:53.220
show up and they dismiss the charges, you
know.

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:58.160
And so that is the kind of thing that for
people that insist on their right to trial,

00:14:58.160 --> 00:14:59.339
they're going to get punished for it.

00:14:59.339 --> 00:15:01.950
And it's what we call the trial penalty is
what happened to Paul Hayes.

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:04.959
It's what's happened to countless clients
that I've had.

00:15:04.959 --> 00:15:08.880
And it's what happens all over America every
single day in those situations.

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:11.550
Speaker 1: I think this was an issue with
with Donziger.

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:17.640
In fact, when the strictest penalty you could
get is less than the time that you've been

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:20.060
detained pretrial.

00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:22.280
Is there some recourse to remedy in those
cases?

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:24.800
Speaker 4: Well, you'd think there would be.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:26.160
Yeah, you'd think that.

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:30.259
And that's that was, you know, the case with
my guy down in eastern Kentucky.

00:15:30.259 --> 00:15:31.259
Yeah.

00:15:31.259 --> 00:15:37.050
But the federal courts are so hostile in most
of the country to these civil rights actions

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:39.230
that you're not very likely to get anywhere.

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:43.690
And especially since this is such a common
thing for people to stay in jail way longer

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:46.260
than they should stay in jail for one reason
or another.

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:47.260
Yeah.

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:51.850
That, you know, most most of those folks that
have been aggrieved by something like that

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:57.560
in the system can't even find a civil lawyer
to represent them to to to vindicate their

00:15:57.560 --> 00:15:58.560
rights.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:02.810
Certainly not in rural parts of the country
where this happens all the time.

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:06.650
And when they do find someone to represent
them, the civil rights actions tend to fall

00:16:06.650 --> 00:16:11.370
flat on their face because you've got these
judges that are hostile to these kinds of

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:12.399
actions to begin with.

00:16:12.399 --> 00:16:13.540
Speaker 1: Right.

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:18.899
The book is pleading out how plea bargaining
creates a permanent criminal class.

00:16:18.899 --> 00:16:22.690
We've been speaking to the book's author,
law professor Dan Cannon.

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:23.690
Dan, thank you so much for your time.

00:16:23.690 --> 00:16:23.700
Speaker 4: Thanks so much for having me.

